-- WAY W4 (not chosen): the versions as DATA.  Each row carries the two dates; one rule reads the two axes once
-- and picks, among the rows that already operate and are already published, the one that operates latest.
--
-- This is the generic lookup (and the shape of the stable row's version table, which has its own tie-break).
-- It gives the same answers as the chosen way for these two versions, is shorter to extend, and is less
-- isomorphic: the Schedule's text is no longer visibly separate from its dating, and "latest operating date wins"
-- is a choice the Act does not state.
-- Printed output is in alt-w4-version-table.out; walkthrough step 7.
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

DECLARE `Version of the First Schedule` IS ONE OF
    `the First Schedule of three items`
    `the First Schedule of the TVRO system only`

DECLARE Row HAS
    `the text`       IS A `Version of the First Schedule`
    `operates from`  IS A DATE
    `published on`   IS A DATE

`the versions table` MEANS LIST
    Row WITH `the text` IS `the First Schedule of three items`,         `operates from` IS Date 31 7 2003, `published on` IS Date 31 7 2003
    Row WITH `the text` IS `the First Schedule of the TVRO system only`, `operates from` IS Date 1 1 2011,  `published on` IS Date 31 10 2012

`the rule-effective day` MEANS `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`
`the reading day` MEANS TODAY

GIVEN row IS A Row
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the row governs` MEANS row's `operates from` AT MOST `the rule-effective day` AND row's `published on` AT MOST `the reading day`

GIVEN best IS A Row
      row IS A Row
GIVETH A Row
`the later-operating of` MEANS IF row's `operates from` GREATER THAN best's `operates from` THEN row ELSE best

`the text, from the table` MEANS
    CONSIDER filter `the row governs` `the versions table`
    WHEN EMPTY                THEN REFUSE "not covered: no version operates and is published"
    WHEN row FOLLOWED BY rows THEN (foldl `the later-operating of` row rows)'s `the text`

GIVEN conduct IS A DATE
      reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Version of the First Schedule`
`the text under this way` MEANS
    `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` reading (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` conduct `the text, from the table`)

-- BEGIN GENERATED PAIRS (build-walkthrough.py: the same conduct-day and reading-day pairs in every file)
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012)   -- P01
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 31 10 2012)   -- P02
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 11 2012)   -- P03
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 1 2020)   -- P04
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 1 8 2012)   -- P05
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 1 11 2012)   -- P06
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 30 10 2012)   -- P07
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 30 10 2012) (Date 1 11 2012)   -- P08
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 31 10 2012) (Date 1 11 2012)   -- P09
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 11 2012) (Date 1 11 2012)   -- P10
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 31 10 2012) (Date 30 10 2012)   -- P11
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 1 11 2012)   -- P12
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 4 11 2026) (Date 4 11 2026)   -- P13
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 30 7 2003) (Date 4 11 2026)   -- P14
#EVAL `the text under this way` (Date 31 7 2003) (Date 4 11 2026)   -- P15
-- END GENERATED PAIRS
